When a beehive swarms, half the colony takes flight to find a new home, taking the existing queen and weeks of productivity with them. For an orchard, this is not just a biological event, it means the remaining pollinating workforce of bees is 1.2 to 2.6 times less efficient at foraging according to [Sandra Kordić Evans, et al](https://www.mdpi.com/2075-4450/17/2/163).

Manual hive checks try to catch swarming early, but visual signs like queen cells often show up when it is already too late to stop the split. Instead of opening boxes and guessing, listening to the hive's acoustic signals provides a direct, predictable way to catch the warning signs early.

## How Hive Sounds Shift

Before bees swarm, the sound inside the hive changes completely as heat increases, thousands of worker bees vibrate in unison, and the calm background hum sharpens into an organized pattern. Running this sound through acoustic analysis converts the audio into clear graph data showing the shift across key frequencies and duration of this shift.

![Cleaned and Processed Frequency Spectrum for Recorded bee data.](/images/blog/bioanalysis-and-honeybees/frequency_spectrum_magma.png)

The graph reveals a primary peak at **242.1 Hz**, representing the fundamental frequency generated by synchronized worker wingbeats and hive ventilation during normal, steady colony activity. Secondary peaks at **494.2 Hz** and **725.0 Hz** appear as integer harmonic overtones. Because these overtones align at precise multiples of the fundamental frequency, they confirm that the signal originates from genuine bee activity rather than environmental noise. This reflects a stable hive baseline.

### What This Means for Your Bottom Line

Instead of sending crews to manually open and inspect every box every week, continuous bioacoustic monitoring tracks these sound signatures automatically and flags specific hives for targeted action the moment they lock into the pre-swarm pattern. This allows field managers to split hives or manage queen cells early, keeping maximum pollinator density in the orchard throughout critical bloom windows. Shifting to targeted checks eliminates wasted labor hours spent inspecting calm, healthy boxes while protecting fruit set and securing higher harvest revenue per hectare.
